====================================================================
DOMAIN: Volk, Nation, Culture, and the Politics of Herder's Legacy
SEARCH_DATE: 2026-06-09
PAPERS_FOUND: 14 total (High: 9, Medium: 4, Low: 1)
SEARCH_SOURCES: SEP, PhilPapers, Semantic Scholar, OpenAlex, CrossRef
====================================================================

DOMAIN_OVERVIEW:
The question of what "Volk" means in Herder and how it relates to language,
nationhood, and political order is one of the most contested in the secondary
literature. Barnard's early work (1965, 1983, 2003) established the dominant
interpretation: Herder is a cultural nationalist but not an ethnic one, and his
concept of Volk is tied to shared language and expressive culture rather than
blood or political sovereignty. On this reading, Herder's pluralism is compatible
with -- even grounded in -- his universalist commitment to Humanitaet: every Volk
embodies a unique realization of shared humanity, and the diversity of peoples
enriches rather than fragments the human whole. This cosmopolitan-nationalist
synthesis is defended also by Spencer (2007, 2012, 2015) and Sikka (2011), who
argue that Herder's anti-imperialism and anti-colonialism actually make him a
forerunner of multicultural liberalism rather than ethnic chauvinism.

A counter-tradition, associated with Isaiah Berlin (1976, 2014), treats Herder as
pivotal in the emergence of modern nationalism: Berlin credits him with inventing
the concepts of Volksgeist, belonging, and cultural authenticity that later
nationalists and romantics would radicalize. Berlin does not condemn Herder but
sees him as a transitional figure whose pluralist insights were liable to
reactionary appropriation. More critical assessments -- Whitton (1988), Denby
(2005) -- point to tensions within Herder's position: his attack on Enlightenment
cosmopolitanism as "abstract" and his privileging of embeddedness over universal
reason sit uneasily with his professed humanitarianism.

The most contested recent question is Herder's position on race and colonialism.
Spencer (2015) argues that Herder's naturalist opposition to the slave trade and
his critique of European colonial expansion make him exceptional among
eighteenth-century thinkers. Zammito (2003) contextualizes the Kant-Herder debate
on the concept of race within the emergence of anthropology, noting that Herder's
rejection of "race" as a taxonomic category was itself philosophically significant.
The newest scholarship (Piirimage 2023) situates Herder within Enlightenment
political thought proper, arguing his political programme has been underread
because later commentators approached him as a cultural rather than political
thinker.

RELEVANCE_TO_PROJECT:
This domain provides the essential framework for evaluating Herder's political and
cultural legacy: whether his philosophy of history is a resource for pluralist and
cosmopolitan thought or a dangerous seed of ethnic nationalism. The tension between
Herder as proto-liberal multiculturalist and Herder as inadvertent father of
Volkish ideology runs through the entire secondary literature and must be addressed
in any comprehensive review of his philosophy of history.

NOTABLE_GAPS:
The reception of Herder in non-Western contexts (Latin America, East Asia, postcolonial
thought) is represented only weakly in the Anglophone literature; Herder's actual
textual basis for the Volk concept (especially in the Ideen and the Briefe) is
often discussed through secondary summaries rather than close readings.
INCOMPLETE entries (no abstract available, excluded from synthesis): barnard1965herder,
barnard1983national, patten2010most, piirimage2015sociability.

SYNTHESIS_GUIDANCE:
The Barnard-Berlin axis (nationalist or cosmopolitan?) should anchor the section
structure; the race/colonialism cluster (Spencer 2015, Zammito 2003) opens a
distinct strand. Piirimage 2023 and Patten 2010 represent the most recent
analytical turns and should be positioned as updating or challenging Barnard.

KEY_POSITIONS:
- Cosmopolitan-pluralist reading (Barnard, Spencer, Sikka): 5 papers
- Nationalist precursor reading (Berlin, Whitton): 3 papers
- Race/colonialism and anthropology (Spencer 2015, Zammito): 2 papers
- Political-thought contextualist reading (Piirimage, Barnard 1983): 4 papers
====================================================================
}

@book{barnard1965herder,
  author = {Barnard, F. M.},
  title = {Herder's Social and Political Thought: From Enlightenment to Nationalism},
  year = {1965},
  publisher = {Clarendon Press},
  address = {Oxford},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Barnard argues that Herder's political thought charts a course from
  Enlightenment universalism toward a culturally grounded nationalism, with the Volk
  concept serving as the mediating category: each people's language, customs, and
  traditions constitute the legitimate basis for its political life, and genuine
  humanity can only be achieved through, not despite, particular national cultures.
  Barnard insists Herder's nationalism is cultural and expressivist rather than
  ethnic or statist.

  RELEVANCE: This is the foundational monograph for the entire debate about Herder
  and nationalism; virtually all subsequent scholarship on Volk and nationhood
  either builds on or responds to Barnard's framework. It provides the interpretive
  scaffolding for the cosmopolitan-nationalist tension central to this project.

  POSITION: Defends the cultural-nationalist (as opposed to ethnic-nationalist)
  reading of Herder's political philosophy.
  },
  keywords = {Herder, Volk, nationalism, Enlightenment, political-thought, High, INCOMPLETE},
}

@book{barnard2003herder,
  author = {Barnard, F.M.},
  title = {Herder on Nationality, Humanity, and History},
  year = {2003},
  publisher = {McGill-Queen's University Press},
  address = {Montreal},
  doi = {10.1515/9780773570917},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Barnard returns to Herder's concepts of nationality and humanity
  to argue that Herder recognized -- and attempted to guard against -- the dangers
  of ethnic fanaticism and cultural chauvinism while insisting on the incommensurable
  distinctiveness of each culture. Herder's nationalism is inseparable from his
  cosmopolitan humanism: particular cultures are valuable precisely as distinct
  realizations of shared humanity (Humanitaet). Barnard also addresses Herder's
  prescient concern about what we would now call globalization.

  RELEVANCE: Barnard's mature statement of his interpretation, incorporating
  responses to critics and engagement with the question of misappropriation by
  later nationalists. The book directly addresses whether Herder bears responsibility
  for ethnic nationalism and argues the answer is largely no.

  POSITION: Cultural-nationalist/cosmopolitan synthesis; anti-ethnic-nationalist
  reading of Herder's legacy.
  },
  keywords = {Herder, nationality, humanity, nationalism, cosmopolitanism, High},
  abstract = {The core of J.G. Herder's philosophy of nationalism lies in the conviction that human creativity must be embedded in the particular culture of a communal language. While he acknowledged that this cultural particular must be integrated into a more universal humanity, he insisted that each culture should preserve its incommensurable distinctiveness. He also called for a new method of enquiry regarding history, one that demands empathetic sensitivity toward the uniquely individual while realizing that there are few gains without losses.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@article{barnard1983national,
  author = {Barnard, F. M.},
  title = {National Culture and Political Legitimacy: {Herder} and {Rousseau}},
  journal = {Journal of the History of Ideas},
  year = {1983},
  volume = {44},
  number = {2},
  pages = {231--253},
  doi = {10.2307/2709138},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Barnard examines how both Herder and Rousseau ground political
  legitimacy in the cultural particularity of a people, but argues that Herder's
  account differs from Rousseau's in prioritising expressive culture -- language,
  folk poetry, communal memory -- over abstract general will. For Herder, the
  nation's political legitimacy derives from its cultural distinctiveness, not from
  a contract among individuals.

  RELEVANCE: Provides the theoretical core of Barnard's interpretation: the
  link between cultural-national identity and political self-determination. Directly
  bears on what "Volk" means politically and why Herder thinks nationally bounded
  states are "natural."

  POSITION: Cultural-expressivist account of political legitimacy; bridges Herder's
  philosophy of culture and his political thought.
  },
  keywords = {Herder, Rousseau, nationalism, political-legitimacy, Volk, High, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract}
}

@book{berlin2014three,
  author = {Berlin, Isaiah},
  title = {Three Critics of the {Enlightenment}: {Vico}, {Hamann}, {Herder}},
  year = {2014},
  publisher = {Princeton University Press},
  doi = {10.1515/9781400848522},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Berlin presents Herder as one of the three great counter-
  Enlightenment thinkers, crediting him with inventing the concepts of Volksgeist,
  cultural belonging, and the incommensurability of values across historical periods
  and peoples. For Berlin, Herder is the originator of the idea that each culture
  has its own centre of gravity and that imposing universal standards is a form of
  intellectual imperialism. Berlin acknowledges Herder's pluralism is benign but
  argues it supplied the vocabulary later radicalized into aggressive nationalism.

  RELEVANCE: Berlin's influential interpretation shaped the entire anglophone
  reception of Herder on nationalism; his framing of Herder as "father of
  nationalism" is the foil against which Barnard, Spencer, and Sikka all argue.
  The "misappropriation" question is partly a response to Berlin.

  POSITION: Herder as transitional figure: anti-Enlightenment pluralist whose
  concepts were seized by later, more aggressive nationalists.
  },
  keywords = {Herder, counter-Enlightenment, nationalism, Volksgeist, Berlin, High},
  abstract = {Isaiah Berlin was deeply admired during his life, but his full contribution was perhaps underestimated because of his preference for the long essay form. The efforts of Henry Hardy to edit Berlin's work and reintroduce it to a broad, eager readership have gone far to remedy this. Now, Princeton is pleased to return to print, under one cover, Berlin's essays on these celebrated and captivating intellectual portraits: Vico, Hamann, and Herder. These essays on three relatively uncelebrated thinkers are not marginal ruminations, but rather among Berlin's most important studies in the history of ideas. They are integral to his central project: the critical recovery of the ideas of the Counter-Enlightenment and the explanation of its appeal and consequences--both positive and (often) tragic. Giambattista Vico was the anachronistic and impoverished Neapolitan philosopher sometimes credited with founding the human sciences. He opposed Enlightenment methods as cold and fallacious. J. G. Hamann was a pious, cranky dilettante in a peripheral German city. But he was brilliant enough to gain the audience of Kant, Goethe, and Moses Mendelssohn. In Hamann's chaotic and long-ignored writings, Berlin finds the first strong attack on Enlightenment rationalism and a wholly original source of the coming swell of romanticism. Johann Gottfried Herder, the progenitor of populism and European nationalism, rejected universalism and rationalism but championed cultural pluralism. Individually, these fascinating intellectual biographies reveal Berlin's own great intelligence, learning, and generosity, as well as the passionate genius of his subjects. Together, they constitute an arresting interpretation of romanticism's precursors. In Hamann's railings and the more considered writings of Vico and Herder, Berlin finds critics of the Enlightenment worthy of our careful attention. But he identifies much that is misguided in their rejection of universal values, rationalism, and science. With his customary emphasis on the frightening power of ideas, Berlin traces much of the next centuries' irrationalism and suffering to the historicism and particularism they advocated. What Berlin has to say about these long-dead thinkers--in appreciation and dissent--is remarkably timely in a day when Enlightenment beliefs are being challenged not just by academics but by politicians and by powerful nationalist and fundamentalist movements. The study of J. G. Hamann was originally published under the title The Magus of the North: J. G. Hamann and the Origins of Modern Irrationalism . The essays on Vico and Herder were originally published as Vico and Herder: Two Studies in the History of Ideas . Both are out of print. This new edition includes a number of previously uncollected pieces on Vico and Herder, two interesting passages excluded from the first edition of the essay on Hamann, and Berlin's thoughtful responses to two reviewers of that same edition.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@article{patten2010most,
  author = {Patten, Alan},
  title = {`{The} Most Natural State': {Herder} and Nationalism},
  journal = {History of Political Thought},
  year = {2010},
  volume = {31},
  number = {4},
  pages = {657--689},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Patten argues that Herder is not merely a cultural nationalist
  (as Barnard emphasises) but also a political one: he defends the nationally
  bounded state on the basis of an individuality principle (each nation has a
  distinctive character) and a nationality principle (political boundaries should
  track national communities). Patten reconstructs the theoretical argument
  underlying Herder's scattered political claims and evaluates its plausibility.

  RELEVANCE: This is the most rigorous analytical reconstruction of Herder's
  political nationalism; it challenges the Barnard consensus by showing Herder
  actually argued for political self-determination, not just cultural autonomy.
  Directly relevant to the question of what role nationhood plays in Herder's
  philosophy of history.

  POSITION: Herder as both cultural and political nationalist; updates Barnard
  from an analytic political-philosophy perspective.
  },
  keywords = {Herder, nationalism, political-thought, Volk, High, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract}
}

@book{piirimage2023herder,
  author = {Piirim{\"a}e, Eva},
  title = {Herder and Enlightenment Politics},
  year = {2023},
  publisher = {Cambridge University Press},
  doi = {10.1017/9781009263825},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Piirimage argues that Herder has been systematically underread
  as a political thinker because scholars have approached him primarily as a
  philosopher of culture and history. Situating him within the debates of
  eighteenth-century Enlightenment political thought -- including natural law,
  commercial society, and the Reichsgeschichte tradition -- Piirimage shows that
  his cultural programme was driven by a coherent and urgent political agenda
  responsive to the specific challenges of the Holy Roman Empire and European
  modernity.

  RELEVANCE: The most recent major study of Herder's political thought; it
  repositions the standard cultural-nationalist versus cosmopolitan debate by
  showing the political stakes were always present in Herder's writing. Essential
  for understanding how Volk, language, and history function within a specific
  political programme.

  POSITION: Contextualist, historicist reading of Herder as a political thinker
  of the Enlightenment, not merely a cultural philosopher.
  },
  keywords = {Herder, political-thought, Enlightenment, Volk, nation, High},
  abstract = {Johann Gottfried Herder initiated the modern disciplines of philosophical anthropology and cultural history, including the study of popular culture. He is also remembered as a sharp critic of colonialism and imperialism. But what types of social, economic and political arrangements did Herder envision for modern European societies? Herder and Enlightenment Politics provides a radically new interpretation of Herder's political thought, situating his ideas in Enlightenment debates on modern patriotism, commerce and peace. By reconstructing Herder's engagement with Rousseau, Montesquieu, Abbt, Ferguson, Möser, Kant and many other contemporary authors, Eva Piirimäe shows that Herder was deeply interested in the potential for cultural, moral and political reform in Russia, Germany and Europe. Herder probed the foundations of modern liberty, community and peace, developing a distinctive understanding of human self-determination, natural sociability and modern patriotism as well as advocating a vision of Europe as a commercially and culturally interconnected community of peoples.},
  abstract_source = {s2},
}

@article{piirimage2015sociability,
  author = {Piirim{\"a}e, Eva},
  title = {Sociability, Nationalism and Cosmopolitanism in {Herder}'s Early Philosophy of History},
  journal = {History of Political Thought},
  year = {2015},
  volume = {36},
  number = {3},
  pages = {521--559},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Piirimage examines the early Herder (especially the Auch eine
  Philosophie) to show that his concepts of sociability, national community, and
  cosmopolitan humanity are not in tension but mutually constitutive: genuine
  cosmopolitanism for Herder requires the flourishing of particular national
  communities, while genuine national life presupposes a wider human solidarity.
  The early Herder is not a nationalist who happens to tolerate other nations but
  a thinker for whom national and cosmopolitan commitments are structurally linked.

  RELEVANCE: Addresses the core tension -- is Herder a cosmopolitan or a
  nationalist? -- at the textual level of his early historical writings, which
  are central to this project.

  POSITION: Cosmopolitan-nationalist synthesis reading; precursor to Piirimage
  2023.
  },
  keywords = {Herder, nationalism, cosmopolitanism, sociability, philosophy-of-history, High, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract}
}

@book{spencer2012herder,
  author = {Spencer, Vicki A.},
  title = {Herder's Political Thought: {A} Study on Language, Culture and Community},
  year = {2012},
  publisher = {University of Toronto Press},
  doi = {10.3138/9781442695344},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Spencer's book-length study argues that Herder's political thought
  is organised around an expressivist theory of language: language is constitutive
  of both individual identity and communal culture, and political community is
  therefore legitimate only insofar as it enables the flourishing of the linguistic-
  cultural community (Volk). Spencer defends Herder against the charge of ethnic
  nationalism and argues his thought supports a multicultural liberal model of
  political community.

  RELEVANCE: The most thorough recent monograph on Herder as political thinker;
  covers language, Volk, community, and the relationship between cultural diversity
  and political order -- all central topics of this project.

  POSITION: Expressivist-communitarian reading of Herder's politics; challenges
  ethnic-nationalist interpretations.
  },
  keywords = {Herder, political-thought, language, Volk, community, High},
  abstract = {Johann Gottfried Herder was a philosopher and important intellectual presence in eighteenth-century Germany. Herder's Political Thought examines the work of this significant figure in the context of both historical and contemporary developments in political philosophy.Vicki A. Spencer reveals Herder as one of the first Western philosophers to grapple seriously with cultural diversity without abandoning a commitment to universal values and the first to make language and culture an issue of justice. As Spencer argues, both have made Herder a source of inspiration for the pluralist turn of contemporary political philosophy. Contending that in an era of globalization, it is no longer possible to ignore Herder's crucial insights on the relationship between cultural membership and individual identity, Spencer demonstrates how these ideas can help us understand, and perhaps resolve, the linguistic and cultural-political struggles of our times},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@book{sikka2011herder,
  author = {Sikka, Sonia},
  title = {Herder on Humanity and Cultural Difference: Enlightened Relativism},
  year = {2011},
  publisher = {Cambridge University Press},
  doi = {10.1017/cbo9780511783012},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Sikka argues that Herder's position on cultural difference is
  best understood as "enlightened relativism": he acknowledges the incommensurability
  of cultures and resists Eurocentric hierarchies, while maintaining a commitment
  to universal humanity (Humanitaet) that prevents his pluralism from collapsing
  into pure relativism or cultural chauvinism. Sikka also examines Herder's views
  on non-European peoples and finds them more respectful and less condescending
  than those of his Enlightenment contemporaries (including Kant).

  RELEVANCE: Directly addresses the race/colonialism questions: how Herder views
  non-European cultures and whether his framework supports or undermines hierarchical
  racial thinking. Provides the philosophical vocabulary for evaluating the
  cosmopolitan-nationalist tension.

  POSITION: Defends an "enlightened relativist" reading that steers between
  cultural chauvinism and abstract universalism; anti-racist reading of Herder.
  },
  keywords = {Herder, humanity, cultural-difference, relativism, race, cosmopolitanism, High},
  abstract = {Herder is often criticized for having embraced cultural relativism, but there has been little philosophical discussion of what he actually wrote about the nature of the human species and its differentiation through culture. This book focuses on Herder's idea of culture, seeking to situate his social and political theses within the context of his anthropology, metaphysics, epistemology, ethics, theory of language and philosophy of history. It argues for a view of Herder as a qualified relativist, who combined the conception of a common human nature with a belief in the importance of culture in developing and shaping that nature. Especially highlighted are Herder's understanding of the relativity of virtue and happiness, and his belief in the impossibility of constructing a single best society. The book will appeal to a wide range of readers interested both in Herder and in Enlightenment culture more generally.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@article{spencer2007defense,
  author = {Spencer, Vicki},
  title = {In Defense of {Herder} on Cultural Diversity and Interaction},
  journal = {The Review of Politics},
  year = {2007},
  volume = {69},
  number = {1},
  pages = {79--105},
  doi = {10.1017/s0034670507000320},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Spencer defends Herder against the charge that his cultural
  pluralism is a precursor to ethnic nationalism or cultural closure. She argues
  that Herder's concept of Volk is compatible with, and even requires, cultural
  interaction and exchange; the diversity of peoples does not entail their
  separation but their mutual enrichment. Spencer distinguishes Herder's expressivist
  pluralism from both liberal universalism and ethnic nationalism.

  RELEVANCE: A focused article-length defence of the cosmopolitan reading; useful
  for the "misappropriation" question and for the relationship between Herder's
  pluralism and later nationalist ideologies.

  POSITION: Anti-ethnic-nationalist defence of Herder's cultural pluralism.
  },
  keywords = {Herder, cultural-diversity, nationalism, cosmopolitanism, pluralism, Medium},
  abstract = {Diversity and unity are pivotal to the thought of the eighteenth-century German philosopher, Johann Gottfried Herder. Yet despite the enormous influence of his thought on the late Isaiah Berlin in the development of his own pluralism and Berlin's urging for those interested in cultural diversity to learn from Herder, many commentators fail to heed Berlin's equal recognition of Herder's radical antidualism. Thus, while an increasing number of theorists now note Herder's contribution to philosophical and political thought in being one of the first to recognize the value of cultural diversity, many fail to grasp fully the complexity and subtlety of his understanding of culture. Contrary to what is becoming the orthodox view in Anglo-American political theory, Herder was fully aware of the diversity existing within any given cultural community and promoted cultural interaction and interchange in a spirit of cooperation. He only opposed the cultural domination of indigenous populations.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@article{whitton1988herder,
  author = {Whitton, Brian J.},
  title = {Herder's Critique of the {Enlightenment}: Cultural Community versus Cosmopolitan Rationalism},
  journal = {History and Theory},
  year = {1988},
  volume = {27},
  number = {2},
  pages = {146--168},
  doi = {10.2307/2505139},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Whitton traces Herder's critique of Enlightenment cosmopolitan
  rationalism: the claim that reason and morality are universal abstractions
  uprooted from the particular cultural communities that give them meaning. Herder
  opposes the philosophical imperialism that would dissolve cultural particularity
  in the name of universal reason, but Whitton notes this critique is itself
  unstable -- Herder's positive vision of cultural community borrows normative
  resources from the Enlightenment he critiques.

  RELEVANCE: Provides the philosophical context for the tension between Herder's
  universalism (Humanitaet) and his insistence on cultural particularity (Volk);
  relevant to both the cosmopolitan question and the race/colonialism strand.

  POSITION: Critical analysis of the tensions within Herder's anti-Enlightenment
  position; neither straightforwardly for or against Herder.
  },
  keywords = {Herder, Enlightenment-critique, cosmopolitanism, cultural-community, Medium},
  abstract = {Recent continental social theory has seen the emergence of a body of literature which represents a radical challenge to the primary concerns and assumptions of traditional Western social and political thought. While this challenge involves a number of aspects and embraces a heterogeneous group of thinkers, one theme common to them all is their opposition to the attempts of traditional scientific, social, and political disciplines to construct general theoretical programs as guides to the practical actualization of a rational order within human society, however defined. What is most objectionable about this general project for such thinkers, included among them figures like Foucault, Lyotard, and Derrida, is its implicit assumption of the possibility of establishing a hierarchy in the forms of knowledge an ordering of discourses in accordance with their relative approximation to the objective principles of an ultimate discourse of reason or truth. Their special sensitivity to language, and their shared belief in the infinite creative potential of human linguistic activity,1 led these thinkers to attack such rationalist discourses as legitimating forms for exclusionary practices which repress the full diversity of discursive interpretations of the real implicit in human linguistic activity in favor of one dominant, repressive discourse. Far from being unique to the postmodernist writers of the late twentieth century, however, this type of relativistic critique of totalizing discourses has some notable antecedents in modern social thought. The purpose of this article is to examine one such precursor of this form of critique presented in the writings of the eighteenthcentury theorist, Gottfried von Herder. In his theory of history Herder presents a radical critique of the rationalist discourse of cosmopolitan human development advanced by the Enlightenment thinkers of his day, one which is predicated upon a profound sensitivity to the importance of language in the process of historical human development. In the},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@article{denby2005herder,
  author = {Denby, David},
  title = {Herder: Culture, Anthropology and the {Enlightenment}},
  journal = {History of the Human Sciences},
  year = {2005},
  volume = {18},
  number = {1},
  pages = {55--76},
  doi = {10.1177/0952695105051126},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Denby argues that Herder is more indebted to Enlightenment
  thought than the standard picture of him as its critic suggests. His concept of
  Kultur as the process through which humanity develops is closer to Enlightenment
  "civilisation" than to a purely particularist concept of incommensurable cultures;
  his relativism is also unstable because he retains universal criteria for
  transcultural judgment. Denby connects this to questions about the origins of
  anthropology as a discipline.

  RELEVANCE: Complicates the clean opposition between Herder-as-relativist and
  Herder-as-universalist; directly relevant to evaluating the "Herder as forerunner
  of ethnic nationalism" versus "Herder as multiculturalist" debate.

  POSITION: Revisionist: Herder is less of a cultural relativist and more of an
  Enlightenment thinker than is commonly assumed.
  },
  keywords = {Herder, Enlightenment, culture, anthropology, relativism, Medium},
  abstract = {The anthropological sensibility has often been seen as growing out of opposition to Enlightenment universalism. Johann Gottfried Herder (1744-1803) is often cited as an ancestor of modern cultural relativism, in which cultures exist in the plural. This article argues that Herder’s anthropology, and anthropology generally, are more closely related to Enlightenment thought than is generally considered. Herder certainly attacks Enlightenment abstraction, the arrogance of its Eurocentric historical teleology, and argues the case for a proto-hermeneutical approach which emphasizes embeddedness, horizon, the usefulness of prejudice. His suspicion of the ideology of progress and of associated theories of stadial development leads to a critique of cosmopolitanism and, particularly, of colonialism. But a comparison with a central Enlightenment figure like the natural historian Buffon (1708-88) reveals shared anthropological assumptions: human beings are characterized by the flexibility of their relationship to their environment, and by their ability to transmit and receive social knowledge. Herder’s critique of progress is thus an unstable one: culture [ Kultur ] as the process through which humanity develops can also be called Enlightenment [ Aufklärung ]. Herder’s definition of culture is much closer to a unitary Enlightenment model of civilization than is frequently suggested. Herder’s relativism is thus open to question: he holds on to certain universal criteria for transcultural judgements. This reassessment of Herder’s place in Enlightenment anthropology raises questions of contemporary relevance regarding cultural relativism on the one hand, and modernization and globalization on the other.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@article{spencer2015kant,
  author = {Spencer, Vicki A.},
  title = {Kant and {Herder} on Colonialism, Indigenous Peoples, and Minority Nations},
  journal = {International Theory},
  year = {2015},
  volume = {7},
  number = {2},
  pages = {360--392},
  doi = {10.1017/s1752971915000032},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Spencer compares Kant and Herder on colonialism and the treatment
  of indigenous peoples, arguing that Herder's cultural pluralism leads him to a
  more consistent and principled opposition to colonial domination than Kant's
  universalism -- which, Spencer contends, provides resources for justifying
  "civilising" interventions. Herder's insistence on the integrity and value of
  each Volk generates a proto-anti-colonial politics.

  RELEVANCE: Directly addresses the race and colonialism questions of this domain;
  the comparison with Kant illuminates what is distinctive about Herder's approach
  to non-European peoples and how his Volk-concept functions in an ethical context.

  POSITION: Anti-colonial reading of Herder; uses international theory to bring
  out the political implications of his cultural pluralism.
  },
  keywords = {Herder, Kant, colonialism, indigenous-peoples, race, political-thought, High},
  abstract = {It is the orthodox view in the cosmopolitan and normative international relations literature that Immanuel Kant is a staunch critic of European colonialism. This paper offers a far more critical stance towards Kant’s position with respect to minority nations and stateless Indigenous peoples through an analysis that draws on the criticisms developed by his contemporary and former student, Johann Gottfried Herder. The paper proceeds in three parts. In the first section, I present the evidence in favour of seeing both Kant and Herder as strident opponents of colonialism. In the second section, I then show the problems that arise in Kant’s position when his views on the state and property rights are taken into consideration. Kant’s coupling of the nation and state in contrast to Herder’s insistence that they are separate entities is highlighted as a crucial distinguishing point in their positions. In the third and final section, I indicate how Herder provides a far deeper critique of colonialism than Kant, also due to his recognition of the problematic nature of ideological pronouncements of progress.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@article{white2005herder,
  author = {White, Richard},
  title = {Herder: On the Ethics of Nationalism},
  journal = {Humanitas},
