  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: White examines the ethical dimensions of Herder's nationalism,
  arguing that Herder provides a morally nuanced account of national identity in
  which membership in a Volk carries genuine ethical significance without licensing
  hostility toward other peoples. White situates Herder's ethics of nationalism
  against both cosmopolitan critics and aggressive nationalist appropriations.

  RELEVANCE: Provides an ethical-philosophical angle on the nationalism debate,
  asking not just whether Herder is a nationalist but whether his nationalism can
  be ethically justified -- relevant to the question of Herder's responsibility
  for later chauvinism.

  POSITION: Moderate ethical defence of Herder's nationalism as a resource for
  moral community without ethnic exclusivism.
